      ],
      "requestAttempts": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "Position also tells us about physical properties. Metals sit on the left and in the middle, with just a few valence electrons. They're shiny, they conduct heat and electricity, and they're malleable, which means they can be hammered into shape. Non-metals sit at the top right. As solids they're dull and brittle, they don't conduct electricity, and many are gases. Along the staircase between them are the metalloids, like silicon. Silicon is shiny like a metal, but brittle like a non-metal, and it conducts electricity only a little. That's why it's used in computer chips.",
